Camoufox: the spoofed viewport is the top window's and follows the page zoom,
and a removed frame's screen is empty.

window.innerWidth/innerHeight and devicePixelRatio come from the config, as
fixed numbers (fingerprint-injection.patch). Measured with camoufox-parity
(2026-10-18/19), three ways that differed from the real values:

- Frames (realms.geometry). The config was applied to every window, so an
  iframe 300 px wide reported the top window's 1280. The configured viewport
  now applies to the top-level window; a frame reports its own box.
- Page zoom (input.scroll.zoom). Firefox's real values follow the page's full
  zoom (Ctrl+wheel, Ctrl+plus, a site's remembered zoom): at 110% a device
  pixel is 55 app units instead of 60, so a 1280x815 viewport is 1173x747 CSS
  px and devicePixelRatio 1.0909. Stock zoomed; Camoufox stayed at 1 and
  1280x815. The spoofed values are now scaled by Firefox's own factor,
  AppUnitsPerDevPixelAtUnitFullZoom / AppUnitsPerDevPixel (1 at 100%).
- Removed frames (realms.retained). A frame's window kept after the frame is
  removed reads an empty screen rect in Firefox (no device context to measure);
  the spoofed screen size answered there as if nothing had happened. The screen
  rects now return empty where Firefox's would.

Still open: the top window's spoofed size stays put when something takes room
from the viewport (an infobar, the find bar), where stock's shrinks.

